;; holder-decision.wat — what `:holder` actually does, and what "O" relaxes. NON-RUNNING; it's
;; here so we can SEE the shapes and decide. No type-theory vocab needed — read the comments.

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; WHAT `:holder` IS (plain): a floor on WHAT KIND of thing is allowed to satisfy the surface.
;;   :holder :Struct  = "you must be an AGGREGATE — a thing with named fields — at least a struct."
;; It exists because a surface can demand FIELDS, and only an aggregate HAS fields.
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

;; ── CASE 1: a FIELD-bearing surface — here `:holder` does REAL work ─────────────────────────
;; :Account DEMANDS a field `balance`. Only an aggregate has fields. So "must be an aggregate"
;; is a REAL, needed requirement. A dialed Peer' has no `balance` field — it SHOULD be rejected.
(:wat::core::defsurface :demo::Account :holder :wat::core::Struct
  :features [(balance <- :wat::core::i64)                       ;; a FIELD member — needs an aggregate
             (deposit [self <- :demo::Account  amt <- :wat::core::i64] -> :wat::core::i64)])
;;   satisfiers: a struct with a `balance` field  ✅   ·   a Peer'  ❌ (correctly — no field)
;;   → for THIS surface, `:holder :Struct` is meaningful. O does NOT change this. Stays mandatory.

;; ── CASE 2: a METHOD-ONLY surface — here `:holder` is VACUOUS but is FORCED on you today ────
;; :Store demands NO fields — only operations (put/scan). NOTHING in it needs an aggregate.
;; But `:holder` is MANDATORY (arc 293 K0a), so you are FORCED to write `:holder :Struct` —
;; a "must be an aggregate" floor that NOTHING in :Store actually needs.
(:wat::core::defsurface :demo::Store :holder :wat::core::Struct   ;; ← the :Struct is VACUOUS but forced
  :features [(put  [self <- :demo::Store  req <- :demo::PutReq] -> :demo::PutResp)   ;; only METHODS,
             (scan [self <- :demo::Store  req <- :demo::ScanReq] -> :demo::ScanResp)]) ;;   no fields
;;   TODAY: a struct satisfies  ✅   ·   a dialed Peer'  ❌ REJECTED — not an aggregate —
;;   even though :Store never asked for an aggregate. The vacuous floor is what blocks the peer.
;;   ↑ THIS is the block. In the service world a caller ALWAYS holds a Peer' (you dial a service),
;;     so this vacuous floor rejects the ONLY thing that ever satisfies a wire contract.

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; OPTION O — a method-only surface may OMIT `:holder` (no fields → no aggregate floor).
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
(:wat::core::defsurface :demo::Store                              ;; ← NO :holder clause
  :features [(put  [self <- :demo::Store  req <- :demo::PutReq] -> :demo::PutResp)
             (scan [self <- :demo::Store  req <- :demo::ScanReq] -> :demo::ScanResp)])
;;   NOW: a struct satisfies  ✅   AND   a dialed Peer' satisfies  ✅   — both work.
;;
;;   WHAT WE RELAX:  the "must declare a holder" rule, ONLY for surfaces that have no fields.
;;                   (a method-only surface stops FORCING "must be an aggregate" — a demand it
;;                    never needed, because it has no fields to require one.)
;;   WHAT WE STILL IMPOSE:  a surface WITH a field member (Case 1) STILL must declare `:holder`.
;;                          Nothing there changes. The floor stays where a field genuinely needs it.
;;   WHAT WE LOSE:  essentially nothing — only the ability to FORCE "aggregates only" onto a
;;                  method-only surface, which was never a meaningful thing to force (no field
;;                  needs it). We give up a restriction that only ever did harm (blocked peers).

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; OPTION V (the alternative) — keep `:holder` ALWAYS mandatory; add a new value `:Value`
;; meaning "any type at all may satisfy" (aggregate or not).
;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
(:wat::core::defsurface :demo::Store :holder :wat::core::Value    ;; ← "anything may satisfy"
  :features [(put  [self <- :demo::Store  req <- :demo::PutReq] -> :demo::PutResp)
             (scan [self <- :demo::Store  req <- :demo::ScanReq] -> :demo::ScanResp)])
;;   ALSO works (struct ✅, Peer' ✅). The awkward part: the holder slot is a ladder of
;;   AGGREGATE KINDS (Struct < Record < HolonRecord). `:Value` is NOT an aggregate kind — it's
;;   "no restriction" — so you're putting "no restriction" into a slot that names a KIND. It reads
;;   fine, but it mixes "which aggregate kind" with "any type" in one slot (two ideas, one place).

;; ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;; THE DECISION IN ONE LINE:
;;   O = "a surface with no fields needs no aggregate-floor; let it omit :holder."   (relax the rule)
;;   V = "always keep the :holder slot; add a 'no restriction' value to it."          (add a value)
;; Both let a Peer' satisfy a wire contract. O drops a floor that was never needed; V keeps the
;; floor slot and adds an escape value. Neither loses the local-struct satisfier.
